    available." None of the three was implemented in SP4a -- Xcp_PIDTable (source/Xcp.c) routed all
    three to Xcp_CmdNotImplemented -- so enabling their keys in a build with the feature gated on
    would have made CONNECT advertise flash programming while all three answered ERR_CMD_UNKNOWN:
    defect D10 verbatim.

    A three-term guard stood here through SP4a and SP4b Task 2, refusing generation rather than
    merely defaulting the keys false -- a default is a trap for whoever flips it, and an integrator
    who set one before its command existed got an explanation here instead of a slave that lies to
    its master. It was written to be self-removing, one term per command as SP4b implemented it:
    PROGRAM_CLEAR's term went first (Task 2, Xcp_DTOCmdPgmProgramClear), and this task's own
    Xcp_DTOCmdPgmProgram and Xcp_DTOCmdPgmProgramMax (source/Xcp_Pgm.c) are what remove the last
    two. The guard itself is gone with them: CONNECT's RESOURCE bit 4 needed all three ctoInfo
    entries enabled at once (Xcp_CTOCmdStdConnect ANDs them), and now that all three commands exist
    there is nothing left for a term to guard. config/xcp.json sets all three keys true.
    test/connect_test.py's test_connect_sets_the_resource_pgm_bit_according_to_enabled_apis is
    DD60's per-conjunct sweep, restored now that it has three real commands to sweep.

    The other two PGM rows (PROGRAM_NEXT 0xCA, GET_PGM_PROCESSOR_INFO 0xCE) were never part of this
    guard, deliberately: they have no configuration key of their own, and nothing reads their ctoInfo
    bit except the dispatcher. It was the CONNECT advertisement that made PROGRAM_CLEAR, PROGRAM and
    PROGRAM_MAX different, and that is also why lifting their own refusal here is what closes D10,
    not merely what implements three more commands.

